 in the sea  dark and desolate ballet is sweltering  summer of the Negro's legitimate  discontent the marvelous new militants  are the great trials and tribulation 'ti  and discipline in a sense we've come to  our nation's capital to cash a check  this isn't the only rhythmically aware  or musical feature of I have a dream  there's another rhetorical device called  anaphora  which King uses even more than  alliteration an opera is the repetition  of a word or phrase at the beginning of  successive sentences something that in  writing would be redundant but in speech  is an effective tool for emphasis and I  have a dream Martin Luther King uses an  afro as an emphasizing tool but also as  an organizing device listen to how these  phrases sketch the larger ideas of the  speech almost like an outline but 100  years 100 years 100 years 100 years like  but we refuse to believe we refuse to  believe now is the time how is the power  is the power is the power we can never  be satisfied and never be setted we  cannot be satisfied we can never be  satisfied I have a dream I have a dream  I have a dream I have a dream I have a  dream to be let freedom ring let freedom  ring let freedom ring freedom ring let  freedom ring the speech goes from the  past to the present to the future of  civil rights and is threaded with a  refusal to settle for anything other  than the ideal of freedom and equality I  think this is a key to Martin Luther  King's rhetorical genius drawing from  his gospel roots he not only uses  alliteration and anaphora even allusion  for dramatic effect but also to help the  audience grasp the structure and  remember the nuance of his message King  knows that for example you may not know  this line from Shakespeare now is the  winter of our discontent made glorious  summer by this son of York but the  phrase will ring familiar it's  sweltering summer of the Negro's  legitimate discontent will not pass  until that is an invigorating autumn of  freedom and equality King shifts the  seasons of course because for those who  live in the American South autumn is  more relieving than summer  know your audience and sticking with  this paragraph for just a moment notice  that this sentence of Shakespearean  poetry is couched in a point about  urgency and not settling a king isn't  all flowery language he draws that image  of paradise then follows it with a  sentence that contains three  easy-to-understand  cliches so to hope that the Negro needed  to blow off steam and will now be  content will have a rude awakening if  the nation returns to business as usual  this mixing of plain and ornate language  is I think a microcosm of what occurs in  the entire speech after alluding to the  Gettysburg Address in the Declaration of  Independence King compares the promises  of those documents to a bad check check  which has come back marked insufficient  funds after invoking the plain realities  of police brutality for whites only  signs and voter suppression he  paraphrases the Bible we will not be  satisfied until justice rolls down like  waters and righteousness like a mighty  stream  the first half of the speech describes  in large part the issue at hand what is  to be done and demanded what is to be  avoided and he tells those who have  suffered to hold fast and believe while  the final part which was famously  improvised by King on the spot rises  into the heights of dreams and ideas I  have a dream that one day every Valley  shall be exalted every Hill and mountain  shall be made low the rough places will  be made plain and the crooked places  will be made straight and the parabola  shall be revealed and all flesh shall  fear together in my video analyzing  Donald Trump's speech I talked about how  he was able to connect with people by  using simple language it's a good  general rule for public speaking when  you're addressing a large diverse  audience the most important part of  rhetoric is being understood Trump does  this by keeping his words basic and  emotional and it works but I think  Martin Luther King offers a counter  example of public speaking one that  more complex and layered in language and  structure but still just as easily  understandable as anything that current  politicians or speakers say and I have a  dream the simple language contextualizes  and grounds the poetic and the poetic  elevates and animates the simple that's  why it echoes across time and will echo  for decades and ages to come something  vital and alive we will be able to speed  up that day when all of God's children  black men and white men Jews and  Gentiles Protestants and Catholics will  be able to join hands and sing in the  words of the old Negro spiritual free at  last free at last hey everybody thanks  for watching and thank you to the great  courses plus for sponsoring this episode  if you love learning for fun which I do  other great courses plus is this  subscription on-demand video learning  service it's got seven thousand online  lecture series from some great Ivy  League and other professors something  that really sort of meshes with what I  was just talking about is a great course  by John McWhorter from Columbia about  the usage of the English language the  the myths about it and it goes into some  of these things about oratory that I  obviously have totally fascinated by  because I think it's organizing the  world anyway if you go to the great  courses plus complex writer you can get  a 30-day free trial and use the offer  code nerd writer I think it's $14.99  after that it's a really cool service if  you just like to learn things which I do  thank you guys so much for watching I  will see all of you not next Wednesday  but I'll be in Boston for nerdcon nerd  bacteria  put the link in description below if  you're in Boston come say hi  [Music]

